            <ArticleTitle>[Assessment of a Chilean Spanish version of the Patient Assessment of Chronic Illness Care Questionnaire].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>869-878</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0034-98872017000700869</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4067/s0034-98872017000700869</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The Patient Assessment of Chronic Illness Care (PACIC) questionnaire is a widely used instrument to assess chronic disease care from a patient's perspective.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To adapt the PACIC questionnaire to Chilean Spanish and to evaluate the psychometric properties of the translation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Descriptive, cross-sectional design with exploratory and confirmatory factor analysis. The PACIC questionnaire was applied, throughout an external client, to 200 users who received health care at the cardiovascular program in a Family Health Center in Concepción, Chile.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The confirmatory factor analysis did not show a good adjustment with the proposed structure in the original instrument. An exploratory factor analysis revealed five factors explaining 58% of total data variability. The distribution of the factor-items of the original questionnaire underwent some modifications, which are explained when analyzing the theoretical construct. A good reliability of the global scale was obtained (Cronbach's α 0.886).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The PACIC questionnaire, Chilean version does not replicate the proposed structure of the original questionnaire. Therefore, further research about its validity, incorporating a higher number or diversity of participants is recommended.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
